1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:06,625
One-to-one means each row on the left partners with at most one row on the right, and the other way too.

2
00:00:06,625 --> 00:00:09,208
A customer has one credentials row.

3
00:00:09,208 --> 00:00:12,250
A credentials row belongs to one customer.

4
00:00:12,250 --> 00:00:15,486
You could put password_hash on customers.

5
00:00:15,486 --> 00:00:18,680
You split it because the facts have a different audience.

6
00:00:18,680 --> 00:00:20,305
Names are widely read.

7
00:00:20,305 --> 00:00:21,555
Hashes are not.

8
00:00:21,555 --> 00:00:26,444
The split is a privacy and permission move as much as a modeling move.

9
00:00:26,444 --> 00:00:31,694
The primary key of credentials is often the same customer_id.

10
00:00:31,694 --> 00:00:34,875
That shared primary key enforces the one.

11
00:00:34,875 --> 00:00:37,680
You cannot insert two hashes for Maya.

12
00:00:37,680 --> 00:00:40,444
The second insert collides on the key.

13
00:00:40,444 --> 00:00:44,777
A unique foreign key also works if the child has its own id.

14
00:00:44,777 --> 00:00:49,652
UNIQUE on customer_id in the child is the one-to-one lock.

15
00:00:49,652 --> 00:00:53,263
Without UNIQUE, you accidentally built one-to-many.

16
00:00:53,263 --> 00:00:59,111
Optional one-to-one uses a nullable unique foreign key, or simply no child row.

17
00:00:59,111 --> 00:01:02,375
A customer without credentials has not signed in yet.

18
00:01:02,375 --> 00:01:03,930
Left join to find them.

19
00:01:03,930 --> 00:01:08,513
Do not fake one-to-one with an application check and a plain foreign key.

20
00:01:08,513 --> 00:01:11,027
Two sessions will insert two children.

21
00:01:11,027 --> 00:01:14,125
Let the unique key refuse the second.

22
00:01:14,125 --> 00:01:17,888
When both sides are required, you have a packaging question.

23
00:01:17,888 --> 00:01:19,819
Maybe it should have been one table.

24
00:01:19,819 --> 00:01:25,305
Split anyway if the columns are wide, secret, or rarely read together.

25
00:01:25,305 --> 00:01:31,680
A shipping profile that every customer must have can still be a second table if the warehouse owns it.

26
00:01:31,680 --> 00:01:34,083
Joins for one-to-one stay simple.

27
00:01:34,083 --> 00:01:40,222
ON credentials.customer_id equals customers.customer_id.

28
00:01:40,222 --> 00:01:42,694
You do not expect a fan-out of rows.

29
00:01:42,694 --> 00:01:47,694
If the result duplicates a customer, your unique constraint is missing.

30
00:01:47,694 --> 00:01:51,875
Name the pair in the diagram as a single bar on both ends.

31
00:01:51,875 --> 00:01:53,222
Say the sentence.

32
00:01:53,222 --> 00:01:56,263
Each customer has at most one credentials row.

33
00:01:56,263 --> 00:01:59,986
Each credentials row belongs to exactly one customer.

34
00:01:59,986 --> 00:02:03,777
If either sentence is wrong, pick a different cardinality.

35
00:02:03,777 --> 00:02:07,763
Vertical split of a wide row is a physical one-to-one.

36
00:02:07,763 --> 00:02:09,291
You still share the key.

37
00:02:09,291 --> 00:02:12,597
Do it when some columns are huge and rarely read.

38
00:02:12,597 --> 00:02:15,402
A biography TEXT might live next door.

39
00:02:15,402 --> 00:02:18,805
Do not split a row because a form had two tabs.

40
00:02:18,805 --> 00:02:20,750
Tabs are not cardinality.

41
00:02:20,750 --> 00:02:25,250
Extension tables that may never exist are optional one-to-one.

42
00:02:25,250 --> 00:02:28,555
A tax-exempt certificate for a few customers.

43
00:02:28,555 --> 00:02:34,375
Do not put nullable certificate columns on every customer if the facts are a bundle.

44
00:02:34,375 --> 00:02:36,513
The bundle is the child table.

45
00:02:36,513 --> 00:02:41,013
Shared primary key makes a join look like an extension of the row.

46
00:02:41,013 --> 00:02:44,555
ORM tools sometimes call this a composed class.

47
00:02:44,555 --> 00:02:47,305
The database still sees two tables.

48
00:02:47,305 --> 00:02:54,597
Keep the foreign key, even if the primary key is shared, so the engine refuses a child with no parent.

49
00:02:54,597 --> 00:02:58,138
A shared key without a foreign key is a rumor.

50
00:02:58,138 --> 00:03:04,000
When both tables grow independently, revisit whether you still mean one-to-one.

51
00:03:04,000 --> 00:03:08,472
If a customer can grow two certificates, the cardinality changed.

52
00:03:08,472 --> 00:03:10,583
Do not ignore the second insert.

53
00:03:10,583 --> 00:03:13,166
UNIQUE will scream if you still claimed one.

54
00:03:13,166 --> 00:03:14,013
Listen.

55
00:03:14,013 --> 00:03:20,000
Change the diagram to one-to-many, or refuse the second certificate in the product.

56
00:03:20,000 --> 00:03:21,458
Either is a decision.

57
00:03:21,458 --> 00:03:23,472
A silent duplicate is not.

58
00:03:23,472 --> 00:03:26,597
Bars on both ends, or a change of shape.

59
00:03:26,597 --> 00:03:28,902
Say it, then lock it with a key.

60
00:03:28,902 --> 00:03:32,875
If you are splitting because a slide had two boxes, stop.

61
00:03:32,875 --> 00:03:36,861
If you are splitting because two audiences exist, continue.

62
00:03:36,861 --> 00:03:41,402
If you are splitting because a column is huge and cold, continue.

63
00:03:41,402 --> 00:03:45,166
If you are splitting and forgetting UNIQUE, you built many.

64
00:03:45,166 --> 00:03:47,555
The last case is the silent one.

65
00:03:47,555 --> 00:03:50,888
Add the unique key the same day as the table.

66
00:03:50,888 --> 00:03:52,416
Join once and count.

67
00:03:52,416 --> 00:03:58,402
The count of customers should match the count of pairs, plus the unmatched optionals.

68
00:03:58,402 --> 00:04:01,833
If it does not, cardinality already drifted.

69
00:04:01,833 --> 00:04:04,916
Fix the constraint before you fix the report.

70
00:04:04,916 --> 00:04:06,708
Reports lie to be helpful.

71
00:04:06,708 --> 00:04:08,347
Constraints refuse.

72
00:04:08,347 --> 00:04:09,777
Prefer refuse.

73
00:04:09,777 --> 00:04:15,958
Cardinality is how many instances of one entity associate with one instance of another.

74
00:04:15,958 --> 00:04:20,500
An entity is the idea; a table is the implementation.

75
00:04:20,500 --> 00:04:25,902
An instance is the idea of one thing; a row is the implementation.

76
00:04:25,902 --> 00:04:31,541
Every relationship has two directions, and each direction gets its own sentence.

77
00:04:31,541 --> 00:04:36,194
A user has zero, one, or more accounts is one direction.

78
00:04:36,194 --> 00:04:39,375
An account belongs to one user is the other.

79
00:04:39,375 --> 00:04:42,791
The subject of the sentence is where the arrow starts.

80
00:04:42,914 --> 00:04:49,044
The object is where the marks sit in crow's foot: near the entity whose count you are stating.

81
00:04:49,044 --> 00:04:54,100
A circle is zero, a bar is one, a crow's foot is many.

82
00:04:54,100 --> 00:04:56,572
Min and max travel as a pair.

83
00:04:56,572 --> 00:05:02,877
One-to-one means each instance on this side matches at most one on that side, and vice versa.

84
00:05:02,877 --> 00:05:09,989
A person and a current passport, if your rules say one live passport per person, can be one-to-one.

85
00:05:09,989 --> 00:05:14,766
A person and a user profile that must not be shared is one-to-one.

86
00:05:14,766 --> 00:05:20,641
Optional one-to-one still uses zero on the min: a user may have no profile yet.

87
00:05:20,641 --> 00:05:29,141
Mandatory one-to-one uses one as the min on both sides, and that is rare because inserts have to create both rows together.

88
00:05:29,141 --> 00:05:35,183
In tables, one-to-one is usually a shared primary key, or a unique foreign key.

89
00:05:35,183 --> 00:05:42,711
If profiles.user_id is UNIQUE and points at users, a user cannot own two profiles.

90
00:05:42,711 --> 00:05:47,489
If you forget UNIQUE, your one-to-one is a one-to-many in costume.

91
00:05:47,489 --> 00:05:53,558
Split a table into one-to-one when some attributes are bulky, secret, or rare.

92
00:05:53,558 --> 00:06:01,141
Addresses, credentials, or medical details might live in a satellite table so the main row stays lean.

93
00:06:01,141 --> 00:06:04,252
Do not split because a screen has two tabs.

94
00:06:04,252 --> 00:06:05,975
Screens are not schemas.

95
00:06:05,975 --> 00:06:16,919
Do not force one-to-one on things the business already treats as many: a customer and their orders is not one-to-one because a second order exists in the English.

96
00:06:16,919 --> 00:06:22,391
Listen for at most one, exactly one, optional extra sheet of facts.

97
00:06:22,391 --> 00:06:24,350
Those phrases are the lesson.

98
00:06:24,350 --> 00:06:26,016
Draw both sentences.

99
00:06:26,016 --> 00:06:30,502
If either sentence says many, you are in a different cardinality.

100
00:06:30,502 --> 00:06:35,891
If both say at most one, put a unique foreign key on one side and stop.

101
00:06:35,891 --> 00:06:37,975
The unique is the enforcement.

102
00:06:37,975 --> 00:06:42,836
The line on the diagram is only a claim until the unique exists.

103
00:06:42,836 --> 00:06:49,572
Q and A for this idea is short on purpose: can this side have two, and can that side have two.

104
00:06:49,572 --> 00:06:52,655
If both answers are no, you have one-to-one.

105
00:06:52,655 --> 00:06:55,558
If you hesitated, you do not have it yet.

106
00:06:55,558 --> 00:06:59,183
Go back to the requirement, not to a prettier diagram.

107
00:06:59,183 --> 00:07:10,086
Implementation patterns: shared primary key, unique foreign key on either side, or a single table if the satellite is always present and tiny.

108
00:07:10,086 --> 00:07:12,002
Single table is allowed.

109
00:07:12,002 --> 00:07:14,905
One-to-one does not require two tables.

110
00:07:14,905 --> 00:07:19,502
Two tables are for optional, bulky, or secret satellites.

111
00:07:19,502 --> 00:07:25,211
If every user has exactly one profile of three columns, keep them on users.

112
00:07:25,211 --> 00:07:31,586
The cardinality can still be described as one-to-one with a person entity if you later split.

113
00:07:31,586 --> 00:07:33,864
Do not split early for purity.

114
00:07:33,864 --> 00:07:37,433
Do split when NULL piles or security says so.

115
00:07:37,433 --> 00:07:49,919
Real-world examples: country and capital, if you model current capital only; person and medical record number issued one-for-one; order and shipment if your shop ships once.

116
00:07:49,919 --> 00:07:52,905
If you ship twice, shipment became many.

117
00:07:52,905 --> 00:07:54,211
Listen for once.

118
00:07:54,211 --> 00:07:59,211
Once is the word that makes this lesson fifteen minutes instead of a definition.

119
00:07:59,211 --> 00:08:03,655
Ask once, at most one, exactly one, optional.

120
00:08:03,655 --> 00:08:05,850
Put the answers on both ends.

121
00:08:05,850 --> 00:08:06,822
Then draw.

122
00:08:06,822 --> 00:08:08,030
Then UNIQUE.

123
00:08:08,030 --> 00:08:10,391
Then join and expect one row.

124
00:08:10,391 --> 00:08:12,211
That sequence is the film.

125
00:08:12,211 --> 00:08:15,850
Close with questions, the way a careful lecture does.

126
00:08:15,850 --> 00:08:18,905
Answer in a sentence you could defend at a keyboard.

127
00:08:18,905 --> 00:08:20,364
What is cardinality?

128
00:08:20,364 --> 00:08:24,072
How many instances associate with one instance of the other.

129
00:08:24,072 --> 00:08:25,627
Why two sentences?

130
00:08:25,627 --> 00:08:28,475
Every relationship has two directions.

131
00:08:28,475 --> 00:08:30,780
What marks mean zero, one, many?

132
00:08:30,780 --> 00:08:33,377
Circle, bar, crow's foot.

133
00:08:33,377 --> 00:08:34,752
What is one-to-one?

134
00:08:34,752 --> 00:08:36,794
At most one on both maxima.

135
00:08:36,794 --> 00:08:38,280
Optional one-to-one?

136
00:08:38,280 --> 00:08:41,530
Min zero on one side, max still one.

137
00:08:41,530 --> 00:08:43,516
How do you enforce it in SQL?

138
00:08:43,516 --> 00:08:47,197
UNIQUE on the foreign key, or a shared primary key.

139
00:08:47,197 --> 00:08:48,850
What if you forget UNIQUE?

140
00:08:48,850 --> 00:08:50,586
It becomes one-to-many.

141
00:08:50,586 --> 00:08:52,697
When do you split a satellite table?

142
00:08:52,697 --> 00:08:57,808
Bulky, secret, or rare facts, not because a screen has tabs.

143
00:08:57,808 --> 00:08:59,405
When do you keep one table?

144
00:08:59,405 --> 00:09:02,030
Tiny attributes always present.

145
00:09:02,030 --> 00:09:04,141
Person and current passport?

146
00:09:04,141 --> 00:09:07,405
One-to-one if the rule is one live passport.

147
00:09:07,405 --> 00:09:09,516
Person and passport history?

148
00:09:09,516 --> 00:09:11,197
One-to-many of events.

149
00:09:11,197 --> 00:09:13,919
Does one-to-one require two tables?

150
00:09:13,919 --> 00:09:14,683
No.

151
00:09:14,683 --> 00:09:16,058
What grain of time?

152
00:09:16,058 --> 00:09:19,627
Current versus history changes the cardinality.

153
00:09:19,627 --> 00:09:21,502
What join fan-out means?

154
00:09:21,502 --> 00:09:23,169
Your unique is missing.

155
00:09:23,169 --> 00:09:25,141
Country and current capital?

156
00:09:25,141 --> 00:09:26,627
Often one-to-one.

157
00:09:26,627 --> 00:09:29,030
Order and shipment if you ship twice?

158
00:09:29,030 --> 00:09:30,239
Many, not one.

159
00:09:30,239 --> 00:09:32,155
What word should you listen for?

160
00:09:32,282 --> 00:09:36,343
Once, at most one, exactly one, optional.

161
00:09:36,343 --> 00:09:38,120
Chen versus crow's foot?

162
00:09:38,120 --> 00:09:40,315
Pick one notation and stay.

163
00:09:40,315 --> 00:09:41,898
Can both mins be one?

164
00:09:41,898 --> 00:09:45,857
Yes, but inserts must create both rows together.

165
00:09:45,857 --> 00:09:48,370
If you hesitate on can this side have two?

166
00:09:48,370 --> 00:09:50,426
You do not have one-to-one yet.

167
00:09:50,426 --> 00:09:55,148
If an answer needed a second table, go draw it before you write more SQL.

